    "scenario": "A corporate calendar system merges overlapping room booking slots into consolidated availability blocks.",
    "code": "import java.util.*;\npublic class Solution {\n    static class Interval { int s, e; Interval(int s, int e) { this.s = s; this.e = e; } }\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);\n        if (sc.hasNextInt()) {\n            int n = sc.nextInt();\n            List<Interval> ints = new ArrayList<>();\n            for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) ints.add(new Interval(sc.nextInt(), sc.nextInt()));\n            ints.sort((a, b) -> a.s - b.s);\n            List<Interval> res = new ArrayList<>();\n            for (Interval interval : ints) {\n                if (res.isEmpty() || res.get(res.size() - 1).e < interval.s) res.add(interval);\n                else res.get(res.size() - 1).e = Math.max(res.get(res.size() - 1).e, interval.e);\n            }\n            for (Interval x : res) {\n                System.out.println(x.s + \" \" + x.e);\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}",
    "explanation": "Interval [1, 3] overlaps with [2, 6] forming [1, 6]. Remaining intervals [8, 10] and [15, 18] do not overlap.",
